Yes. If you already have **file-path inventory**, the next accuracy jump should be **dependency/context awareness**, not just adding more files.

I would build LoopGoal in this order:

### 1. File inventory → File relevance graph

Knowing every path is good:

```text
src/api/user.go
src/service/user.go
src/repository/user.go
tests/user_test.go
```

But LoopGoal should determine **why each file matters**:

```text
Requirement: Add user authentication

user.go
 ├─ imported by → auth.go
 ├─ used by → user_service.go
 ├─ tested by → user_test.go
 └─ configured by → auth.config
```

So the system knows the **impact surface**, not just the path.

---

### 2. Dependency graph

Next scan:

```text
File
 ↓
Imports
 ↓
References
 ↓
Callers
 ↓
Tests
 ↓
Configuration
 ↓
Related docs
```

This is much more valuable than simply asking an LLM:

> “Which files should I modify?”

The deterministic scanner can discover much of this itself.

---

### 3. Symbol-level mapping

Go one level deeper than files.

Instead of:

```text
user_service.go
```

track:

```text
UserService.CreateUser()
UserService.DeleteUser()
UserService.GetUser()
```

Then:

```text
Requirement
   ↓
Task
   ↓
Symbol
   ↓
File
   ↓
Tests
```

This makes large repositories much easier to reason about.

---

### 4. Requirement → Evidence graph

This is probably your **biggest next feature**.

Every requirement should have evidence.

```text
REQ-001
"User can register"

Evidence:
✓ src/api/auth.go
✓ src/service/user.go
✓ tests/auth_test.go
✓ test command passed
✓ API response verified
```

Never:

```text
REQ-001
Status: DONE
Reason: Agent said done
```

Instead:

```text
REQ-001
Status: VERIFIED
Evidence: 6 items
```

---

### 5. Change-impact analysis

Before the agent edits anything:

```text
Expected changes:
  8 files
```

Afterward:

```text
Actual changes:
  11 files
```

LoopGoal asks:

```text
+3 unexpected files

Why?
```

Similarly:

```text
Expected: 8
Actual: 5

3 expected files untouched
```

That should automatically reopen the task.

This is extremely useful.

---

### 6. Rules → actual code compliance

Don't just load:

```text
AGENTS.md
```

Convert rules into checkable requirements.

Example:

```text
Rule:
"Controllers must not contain business logic."

Mapping:

Controller → Business logic detected
Status: FAIL
```

So:

```text
Rules
 ↓
Constraints
 ↓
Affected files
 ↓
Verification
```

---

### 7. Test coverage mapping

Don't simply run:

```text
tests passed
```

Ask:

```text
Which requirements do these tests prove?
```

Example:

```text
REQ-001 → test_auth_login ✓
REQ-002 → test_refresh_token ✓
REQ-003 → no evidence ✗
```

Then:

```text
ALL TESTS PASS
```

does **not** automatically mean:

```text
TASK COMPLETE
```

---

### 8. Semantic repository search

Add a repository knowledge layer:

```text
User request
      ↓
Exact paths
      ↓
Dependencies
      ↓
Symbols
      ↓
Semantic search
      ↓
Related docs/config/tests
```

The LLM should receive a **small relevant context**, not the entire repository.

This also reduces token cost.

---

### 9. Continuous discovery

This is critical.

Suppose the agent changes:

```text
src/auth/login.ts
```

and that reveals:

```text
src/auth/session.ts
```

LoopGoal should automatically do:

```text
CHANGE DETECTED
      ↓
RESCAN
      ↓
NEW RELEVANT FILE
      ↓
ADD TO TASK GRAPH
      ↓
VERIFY
```

So your graph is **dynamic**, not created once at the beginning.

---

## The architecture I'd aim for

```text
                 USER GOAL
                    │
                    ▼
             REQUIREMENT GRAPH
                    │
                    ▼
              TASK GRAPH
                    │
        ┌───────────┼───────────┐
        ▼           ▼           ▼
   FILE GRAPH   SYMBOL GRAPH  RULE GRAPH
        │           │           │
        └───────────┼───────────┘
                    ▼
              AGENT EXECUTION
                    │
                    ▼
              CHANGE DETECTION
                    │
                    ▼
             IMPACT ANALYSIS
                    │
                    ▼
              RE-RECONCILIATION
                    │
                    ▼
              VERIFICATION
                    │
                    ▼
             EVIDENCE GRAPH
                    │
                    ▼
             COMPLETION GATE
```

### If I were choosing your **next 5 improvements**

**1. Dependency/impact graph** ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
**2. Requirement → evidence mapping** ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
**3. Symbol-level mapping** ⭐⭐⭐⭐
**4. Expected-vs-actual change analysis** ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
**5. Continuous discovery/reconciliation** ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

The key evolution is:

> **File paths tell LoopGoal what exists. Dependency + symbol + evidence graphs tell LoopGoal what matters and whether the work is actually complete.**

That is the direction I'd take before adding lots of agent integrations or fancy UI.
